A mutation probe for the over-release, and it takes 5 in 16 to 0 in 16.

Reading eliminated six suspects and named none, so this changes OWNERSHIP and nothing else: one
extra retain on the resolved colour, which only an over-release somewhere else could consume. Off
unless CIDER_COLOR_EXTRA_RETAIN is set, and the getenv runs in BOTH arms so the timing either
side of the comparison is the same. That matters here more than usual, because every ordinary
instrument suppresses this bug: a colour trace gives 0 in 8 and zombies give 0 in 8.

MEASURED:

  no extra retain, previous build   3 of 8 died
  no extra retain, THIS build       2 of 8 died      <- the build still reproduces it
  extra retain, THIS build          0 of 16 died

Pooled that is 5 of 16 against 0 of 16. At a 31 percent base rate the chance of 0 in 16 by luck
is about 0.3 percent. The middle row is the control that matters: the same binary, the same
runs, only the environment variable different, so the rebuild is not what changed the rate.

WHAT IT MEANS AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. It is a DIAGNOSTIC, not a fix, and it must not be turned on
by default: an unconditional extra retain leaks a colour per catalog entry. What it establishes
is that something releases the resolved colour once too often, which is where the search goes
next, and that the catalog colour is the VICTIM rather than the culprit. Its own retain is
genuine: NSColor copyWithZone is "return [self retain]" and there is exactly one copyWithZone in
the whole NSColor subproj, so no subclass changes that.

The guards from 0040 say the entry is a valid colour when STORED and bad at USE, so the window
is between those two points. See task #227.

--- a/AppKit/NSColor.subproj/NSColor_catalog.m
+++ b/AppKit/NSColor.subproj/NSColor_catalog.m
@@ -45,6 +45,17 @@
     _color = [color copy];
 
     /*
+     * A MUTATION PROBE FOR THE OVER-RELEASE, off unless asked for. Reading has eliminated six
+     * suspects and named none, so this changes OWNERSHIP and nothing else: an extra retain that
+     * only an over-release could consume. The getenv runs in both configurations, so the timing
+     * either side of the comparison is the same, which matters because every ordinary trace takes
+     * the failure rate to zero. If the rate goes 3 in 8 to 0 in 8 with this set, something
+     * releases a colour it does not own and the search moves to who. See task #227.
+     */
+    if (getenv("CIDER_COLOR_EXTRA_RETAIN") != NULL)
+        [_color retain];
